%erg 0.1
Title: R17/R18: a silent fallback to keyword-only is an answer lying about what produced it
Created: 2026-09-03
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-03T10:54Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

R31 was retired on 2026-09-03 (`DECISIONS.md`). Its ruling states that the harm
it was reaching for is real, names a live instance, assigns the residue to R17,
R18 and R34 — and files nothing. The review of PR #266 called that out, and it
is right: the inheritance is asserted rather than made real, and the retirement
removed the one visible, numbered, ladder-tracked placeholder that had been
keeping this concrete defect from disappearing quietly.

The instance, in the adapter's own words
(`bench/acceptance/adapters/zoteus.py`): without being pointed at a model
runtime the target **falls back to keyword-only, silently**, and "answered
semantically" and "fell back to keyword-only" are *the same file and different
facts*. A user whose semantic lane is down gets results that look like results.
That is R18's failure mode one level up — R18 makes an *empty* answer say which
emptiness it is; nothing yet makes a *degraded* answer say it is degraded — and
R17's "name the execution device actually serving" is the surface where it
should be visible, since a user who cannot see that cannot explain the answers
they get.

Note what this ticket is NOT. It is not R31 under a new number: R31 asked *when*
a configuration is validated, which three assertion attempts each failed to
distinguish from R10. This asks whether an answer discloses what produced it,
which is a property of the reply, observable at the boundary, and belongs to
promises that already exist.

## Relevant files

- `SPEC.md` R17 and R18 — the two promises this defect falls between; R17's
  device clause and R18's three disjoint sentences.
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.6 — where the disclosure sentences and the typed degradation
  labels (`CROSS_LINGUAL_DEGRADED`, `CJK_KEYWORD_DEGRADED`) live. A fourth
  condition may belong beside them rather than in new prose.
- `bench/acceptance/adapters/zoteus.py` — the declaration that records the
  fallback, and the `status()` that reads whoami plus index status precisely
  because "a configuration that says local and a process that fell back to
  keyword-only are the same file and different facts".
- `bench/acceptance/assertions.py` — where an assertion would go.
- `DECISIONS.md`, 2026-09-03, R31's retirement — the ruling that owes this
  ticket its existence.

## Actions

1. Decide the address before writing anything: is this a clause of R18
   (an answer discloses which kind it is), of R17 (status names what is
   actually serving), or a typed disclosure beside §5.2.6's two existing ones?
   The ledger already carries an open question of exactly this shape for
   budget-exhausted answers, raised 2026-09-03 against ticket 0590 — the two
   should be ruled together or explicitly kept apart, because both ask whether
   a *degraded* answer needs a sentence R18 does not currently reach.
2. Assert it over the seven verbs. The harness controls whether the model
   runtime path is supplied, so the degraded state is reachable on demand —
   which is what makes this decidable where R31 was not.
3. Red first: a fail-control whose query answers from the keyword lane while
   reporting nothing about it must go red, or the assertion has not shown it
   can detect the defect.

## Test

Drive `configure` and `query` through the adapter with no model runtime
supplied, then read the reply and `status`. The assertion is red when an answer
comes back with no indication that the semantic lane did not serve it. Run it
first against the supplied-runtime arm as the discriminating control: if it
reddens there too, it is grading something else.

## Verification

- [ ] the assertion reddens with no runtime supplied and passes with one, on
      the same target and the same query
- [ ] the state it reports is distinguishable from `not-offered` and `not-run`
- [ ] the clause it names exists in `SPEC.md` under R17, R18 or §5.2.6, and the
      ruling that put it there is in `DECISIONS.md`

## Invariants

- No new requirement number. R31 is retired and never reused, and this defect
  is a clause of promises that already exist — minting a number for it would
  re-run the mistake this ticket is the consequence of.
- The adapter declares and does nothing else: the fallback is observed through
  the target's own surfaces, never inferred from a path this repository knows.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] the address is ruled and the clause is written where the ruling puts it
- [ ] an assertion exists, seen red against a fail-control before green
- [ ] `make check` green; `./tickets/erg check tickets/` green
